<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P06_Ex5_Factor_Step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>In the exercise above, \(x\) and \(y\) are different integers.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Complete the line below.</p>
<div>\(x^{k+1} - y^{k+1} = x\left(x^k - y^k\right) +\) [[input:ans1]] \((x - y)\) [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Now assume that \(x^k - y^k = (x - y)q\) for some integer \(q\). Complete the line below. Give your answer in terms of \(x\), \(y\), \(k\) and \(q\).</p>
<div>\(x^{k+1} - y^{k+1} = (x - y) \times \Big(\) [[input:ans2]] \(\Big)\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>The trick is to add and subtract \(xy^k\), so that \(x^k - y^k\) appears:</p>
<p>\[x^{k+1} - y^{k+1} = x^{k+1} - xy^k + xy^k - y^{k+1} = x\left(x^k - y^k\right) + y^k(x - y).\]</p>
<p>So the missing factor is \(y^k\).</p></li>
<li><p>By the induction hypothesis, \(x^k - y^k = (x - y)q\), so</p>
<p>\[x^{k+1} - y^{k+1} = x(x - y)q + y^k(x - y) = (x - y)\left(xq + y^k\right).\]</p>
<p>Since \(x\), \(y\) and \(q\) are integers, \(xq + y^k\) is an integer, so \(x^{k+1} - y^{k+1}\) is divisible by \(x - y\). Splitting the other way, \(x^{k+1} - y^{k+1} = y\left(x^k - y^k\right) + x^k(x - y)\), gives \((x - y)\left(yq + x^k\right)\), which is just as good.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
